WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.533
As with lists and stacks

00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:06.107
we first say what a binary tree can do

00:00:06.107 --> 00:00:07.804
and only then how.

00:00:07.804 --> 00:00:11.377
The binary-tree ADT has six operations.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.879
Create makes an empty tree

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.380
and isEmpty tests for one.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.835
makeBT takes two trees and an element

00:00:19.913 --> 00:00:22.087
and returns a new tree whose

00:00:22.165 --> 00:00:23.950
root holds the element

00:00:24.028 --> 00:00:27.056
with the first tree as its left subtree

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.307
and the second as its right.

00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:32.257
leftChild, rightChild and data take

00:00:32.335 --> 00:00:33.810
a tree apart again.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.313
Those three report an error

00:00:36.393 --> 00:00:39.742
on the empty tree, because it has no root.

00:00:39.742 --> 00:00:42.614
So makeBT builds trees bottom-up

00:00:42.614 --> 00:00:44.687
first the small subtrees

00:00:44.687 --> 00:00:46.920
then a root on top of them.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.465
There are two standard implementations

00:00:50.465 --> 00:00:52.594
linked nodes, and an array.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:54.723
We start with linked nodes.

00:00:54.723 --> 00:00:57.916
Each node holds four fields: the element

00:00:57.916 --> 00:00:59.969
a reference to its parent

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:02.097
which is null for the root

00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:04.454
and references to the roots of

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.659
its left and right subtrees

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.940
null when a subtree is empty.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.357
A tree is then simply a reference

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.030
to its root node

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.632
and the empty tree is null.

00:01:16.632 --> 00:01:18.863
So create returns null

00:01:18.863 --> 00:01:21.930
and isEmpty compares with null.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.398
Here is makeBT with linked nodes.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.378
It allocates a new root

00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:29.194
node for the element.

00:01:29.194 --> 00:01:31.834
The new root has no parent yet

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.732
so its parent is null.

00:01:33.732 --> 00:01:36.702
It hangs the two subtrees under it

00:01:36.702 --> 00:01:39.590
as its left child and right child.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.533
Then it points the subtrees' parent

00:01:42.607 --> 00:01:44.904
fields back up at the new root

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.608
if they are not empty.

00:01:46.608 --> 00:01:48.460
And it returns the root.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.783
Every operation of the ADT does

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:54.371
a constant amount of work

00:01:54.371 --> 00:01:56.385
so each is O(1).

00:01:56.481 --> 00:01:59.549
Building a tree of n nodes takes

00:01:59.645 --> 00:02:02.330
n calls to makeBT: O(n).

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.437
The demo builds this tree

00:02:05.437 --> 00:02:07.866
A with children B and D

00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:10.101
E the left child of B

00:02:10.101 --> 00:02:12.822
and I the right child of D.

00:02:12.822 --> 00:02:15.251
It starts at the bottom.

00:02:15.348 --> 00:02:18.458
Leaf E is makeBT with E in the

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:21.470
middle and null on both sides.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.169
Then B, with E as its left subtree and

00:02:25.257 --> 00:02:27.284
an empty right subtree.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:29.752
B's left child refers to E

00:02:29.752 --> 00:02:32.660
and E's parent refers back to B.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.430
D is built the same way

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.656
with a new leaf I on the right.

00:02:38.656 --> 00:02:40.370
I's parent is D.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.880
Finally, A on top of B and D.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.149
Now B and D get their parent

00:02:47.149 --> 00:02:48.892
the new node A.

00:02:48.892 --> 00:02:53.360
The variable t refers to the whole tree.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.221
The program then asks the

00:02:56.312 --> 00:02:58.137
tree some questions.

00:02:58.137 --> 00:02:59.506
The root is A.

00:02:59.506 --> 00:03:02.517
The left child of the root is B.

00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:04.980
The right child of D is I.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:08.598
Following parent links from E

00:03:08.598 --> 00:03:12.629
up and up again, gives B and then A.

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:15.570
Size 5, and height 2.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.112
B's right subtree is empty

00:03:18.112 --> 00:03:20.224
so isEmpty returns true.

00:03:20.305 --> 00:03:23.228
And asking for the data of the empty

00:03:23.309 --> 00:03:25.420
tree throws the exception

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.775
with the message empty tree.

00:03:27.775 --> 00:03:29.480
The ADT says: error.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.280
Now predict.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.432
The node b was built before t.

00:03:34.432 --> 00:03:37.075
Right after b is created

00:03:37.075 --> 00:03:39.312
what is b.parent?

00:03:39.312 --> 00:03:41.853
And when does it change?

00:03:41.853 --> 00:03:44.700
Pause the video and decide.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.981
makeBT sets the new root's parent to null

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:54.504
so right after it is created

00:03:54.504 --> 00:03:56.354
b.parent is null.

00:03:56.354 --> 00:04:00.054
It changes in the last call, makeBT(b,

00:04:00.054 --> 00:04:03.166
"A", d), which runs

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:04.680
t1.parent = rootNode.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.972
Every subtree gets its parent at the moment

00:04:08.041 --> 00:04:10.230
something is built on top of it.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.334
Nested makeBT calls get long

00:04:13.415 --> 00:04:15.287
so from now on we write

00:04:15.369 --> 00:04:18.381
a binary tree as text: a label, then

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:21.800
in brackets, its left and right subtrees

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.590
separated by a comma.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.847
An empty subtree is written as nothing.

00:04:26.847 --> 00:04:30.510
So B(E,)

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.010
is B with left child E and no right child.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.418
The whole tree is

00:04:38.418 --> 00:04:40.447
A(B(E,),D(,I)).

00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:44.391
The class TreeText turns such text

00:04:44.504 --> 00:04:49.238
into a tree with makeBT, and back again.

00:04:49.238 --> 00:04:53.859
The demo prints the text back, and says

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:58.030
size 5, height 2, and not full

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.200
because B and D each have one child.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.211
Now the second implementation: an array.

00:05:06.297 --> 00:05:09.042
Number the positions of a binary

00:05:09.128 --> 00:05:12.130
tree level by level, left to right

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.789
starting from 1 at the root.

00:05:14.789 --> 00:05:18.650
The root is 1, its children 2 and 3,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.909
their children 4, 5, and so on.

00:05:21.909 --> 00:05:24.740
Call this number the node's ID.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.748
With this numbering,

00:05:26.748 --> 00:05:29.899
moving around the tree is arithmetic.

00:05:29.899 --> 00:05:32.884
Node i has its left child at 2i

00:05:32.884 --> 00:05:36.118
and its right child at 2i + 1.

00:05:36.118 --> 00:05:39.849
B is 2, so its children are 4 and 5.

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:42.585
And its parent is at i/2

00:05:42.585 --> 00:05:45.570
rounded down: 5/2 is 2.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.134
So we can store the tree in an array

00:05:49.134 --> 00:05:52.434
with the element of node i in cell i.

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:54.693
Slot 0 is left unused

00:05:54.693 --> 00:05:58.080
so that the formulas stay this simple.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.894
C is node 3;

00:05:59.894 --> 00:06:03.281
its children would be 6 and 7,

00:06:03.281 --> 00:06:06.393
but the tree has only five nodes,

00:06:06.393 --> 00:06:07.766
so C has none.

00:06:07.857 --> 00:06:10.878
A complete tree with n nodes uses

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:14.630
exactly the IDs 1 to n, with no gaps.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.056
The same numbering works

00:06:17.132 --> 00:06:18.670
for any binary tree:

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.823
every node gets the ID of its position,

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.745
and the slots of missing

00:06:23.822 --> 00:06:25.437
positions stay empty.

00:06:25.437 --> 00:06:27.744
In this tree from the slides,

00:06:27.744 --> 00:06:31.050
B has a left child, E, but no right child.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.313
In code,

00:06:32.313 --> 00:06:36.992
the IDs are worked out on the way down:

00:06:36.992 --> 00:06:39.160
the root gets 1,

00:06:39.274 --> 00:06:42.925
and a node with ID i passes

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.919
2i to its left child

00:06:47.034 --> 00:06:49.658
and 2i + 1 to its right child.

00:06:49.658 --> 00:06:54.451
A is 1, B 2, E 4, K 8, L 9;

00:06:54.451 --> 00:06:57.760
D 3, H 6, and I 7.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.781
Slot 5, B's missing right child,

00:07:00.781 --> 00:07:01.773
stays empty.

00:07:01.773 --> 00:07:03.909
Nine slots for eight nodes

00:07:03.909 --> 00:07:07.190
the array prints exactly as the note says.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.323
But the array needs as many

00:07:10.417 --> 00:07:12.781
slots as the largest ID

00:07:12.781 --> 00:07:15.428
not as the number of nodes.

00:07:15.523 --> 00:07:18.359
A chain that always goes right

00:07:18.454 --> 00:07:22.330
uses the IDs 1, 3, 7, 15.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.410
The program Skewed prints it

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.328
four nodes need 15 slots

00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:31.340
and six nodes need 63.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.788
In general, 2^n − 1

00:07:33.788 --> 00:07:36.800
slots for n elements.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.579
And makeBT is no longer cheap

00:07:39.579 --> 00:07:42.083
every node moves one level down

00:07:42.083 --> 00:07:43.601
so its ID changes

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:46.410
and two whole arrays must be copied.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.535
O(n) instead of O(1).

00:07:48.611 --> 00:07:50.812
That is why the array is used

00:07:50.888 --> 00:07:52.330
for complete trees

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:54.455
where it wastes nothing and

00:07:54.531 --> 00:07:56.428
needs no pointers at all.

00:07:56.428 --> 00:07:59.160
You will meet it again in the heap

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.830
in lecture 17.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.786
The numbering gives a one-line

00:08:03.865 --> 00:08:05.607
test for completeness

00:08:05.686 --> 00:08:08.774
a tree with n nodes is complete exactly

00:08:08.853 --> 00:08:11.229
when its largest ID equals n.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:13.842
Any gap pushes some ID above n.

00:08:13.842 --> 00:08:16.930
Here are the four trees from part one.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.216
The first has six nodes

00:08:19.216 --> 00:08:22.764
but its largest ID is 7: not complete.

00:08:22.845 --> 00:08:24.781
The third has four nodes

00:08:24.861 --> 00:08:27.362
and largest ID 4: complete

00:08:27.362 --> 00:08:29.378
although it is not full.

00:08:29.378 --> 00:08:32.120
The same answers we found by eye.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:33.513
To sum up.

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:37.171
One: the binary-tree ADT has six operations

00:08:37.171 --> 00:08:40.423
and with linked nodes each is O(1).

00:08:40.423 --> 00:08:42.943
Two: a node stores its element

00:08:43.025 --> 00:08:45.382
and three references, parent

00:08:45.382 --> 00:08:47.659
left child and right child

00:08:47.659 --> 00:08:50.666
and makeBT builds trees bottom-up

00:08:50.666 --> 00:08:52.780
setting the parent links.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.783
Three: in an array

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.132
node i has its children at

00:08:57.219 --> 00:08:59.393
2i and 2i + 1

00:08:59.393 --> 00:09:02.003
and its parent at i/2.

00:09:02.003 --> 00:09:04.960
That is exact for complete trees

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.570
and wasteful for skewed ones.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:08.882
Try this.

00:09:08.882 --> 00:09:11.340
Draw the array of

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.653
1(2(,4(8,)),

00:09:14.653 --> 00:09:16.683
3(5,)).

00:09:16.683 --> 00:09:19.142
Which slots are empty

00:09:19.142 --> 00:09:21.920
and is the tree complete?

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.692
In part 3

00:09:23.692 --> 00:09:27.855
we visit every node of a tree: preorder

00:09:27.855 --> 00:09:30.190
inorder and postorder.

